.TH AVR\-REFERENCE 1 "" "avr" "Avrea CLI"
.SH NAME
avr-reference \- List avr's commands, options and JSON fields.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B avr reference
[OPTIONS] [COMMAND]...
.SH DESCRIPTION
List avr's commands, options and JSON fields.
.PP
Lists every command, or those below the COMMAND words given, from the same data as the reference documentation. The words may be aliases, such as groups. With --json each row holds a command's help, its options and arguments, and the fields its --json accepts, so a script or coding agent can learn avr without reading help text. It needs no sign-in and makes no requests.
.PP
Without COMMAND words the first row is avr itself, with its own options. Each is marked inherited when every command takes it too.
.PP
json_fields lists a command's default fields. It is empty where the fields depend on the credential or platform, as for auth status and org install add, and an option such as tests list --summary or secret get --version-id selects others. The command with --json ? lists the fields of that invocation.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
Examples:
    avr reference
    avr reference group member
    avr reference --json command,json_fields --jq '.[] | select(.json_fields | length > 0)'
.fi
.RE
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
JSON FIELDS
    command, deprecated, help, is_group, json_fields, params, short_help,
    usage
.fi
.RE
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
[\fICOMMAND...\fR]
\&
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR \fITEXT\fR
Output JSON. Pass comma-separated field names, "*" for all fields, or "?" to list available fields.
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-jq\fR \fITEXT\fR
Filter --json output through a jq expression.
